-- title:
--   Toric ⊆ identity-reducing ⊆ finite part; finiteness of J[p]^f
-- statement:
--   Throughout, $p$ is an odd prime, $M$ is a positive integer with $p \mid M$ and $p^2 \nmid M$, and $H \le (\mathbb{Z}/M)^{\times}$ is a subgroup; the hypothesis `hHp` requires every unit of $\mathbb{Z}/M$ whose image in $(\mathbb{Z}/(M/p))^{\times}$ is trivial to lie in $H$. A set of primes $S \subseteq \mathbb{N}$ is fixed, and `hin` is the conjunction of the Hecke and diamond inputs `HeckeDiamondInputsHAll M H`: for every prime $\ell$ the condition `HeckeInputsHAlong` over $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ for level $(M,H)$, and for every $d \in (\mathbb{Z}/M)^{\times}$ the existence of a $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$-algebra automorphism $\sigma$ of the function field $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}\cdot F_H(M)$ with `IsDiamondAutHBar M H d σ`. The Jacobian in play is $J = J_H(M) = \operatorname{Pic}^0$ of that function field, and $T_p J$ is the Tate module [`TateModule p (JH M H)`](def/EllipticCurve_TateModule.html#L15), the group of sequences $(x_n)$ in $J$ with $p^n x_n = 0$ and $p\,x_{n+1} = x_n$.
--
--   Hecke data. A commutative ring $\mathbb{T}$ is given, an algebra over $\mathbb{Z}_p$ acting on $T_p J$ compatibly with the $\mathbb{Z}_p$-action; `hfaith` says the action is faithful, `op` assigns to each generator $g$ of type [`CohCarrier.Gen M S`](def/CohCarrier_Inst.html#L13) (the generators $T_\ell$ for primes $\ell \notin S$ with $\ell \nmid M$, $U_q$ for primes $q \mid M$, and $\langle d\rangle$ for $d \in (\mathbb{Z}/M)^\times$) an element $\mathrm{op}(g) \in \mathbb{T}$; `hop` says $\mathrm{op}(g)$ acts on $T_p J$ as `tateGenOpH`, the endomorphism induced by `genOpH` on the Tate module, and `hgen` says the $\mathbb{Z}_p$-subalgebra generated by the range of $\mathrm{op}$ is all of $\mathbb{T}$. Further, $S'$ is an idempotent splitting of $\mathbb{T}$ (a finite family of complete orthogonal idempotents $e_i$ together with an exhaustive list of the maximal ideals $\mathfrak{m}_i$, with $e_i \in \mathfrak{m}_j$ exactly for $i \ne j$), $i_0$ is an index, and `hord` requires $\mathrm{op}(U_p) \notin \mathfrak{m}_{i_0}$.
--
--   Place and geometric models. $\mathfrak{P} =$ `Pl` is a valuation subring of $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ with $p$ a non-unit of $\mathfrak{P}$ (`hPl`), whose residue field has characteristic $p$ and is algebraically closed. The hypothesis `hj` places the $q$-expansion of $j$ in the field `qExpFunctionFieldC ℚ ⊤`; $\mathfrak{X}$ is an integral model `XHDRModelAtP` for the Deligne–Rapoport data at $p$ for level $(M,H)$, $\Lambda$ is a `LevelData` at level $\Gamma_H(M/p)$-type data over $\mathfrak{P}$, `hΛ` asserts the bundle of abelian-scheme properties (smoothness, properness, connected fibres, existence of a relative group law) for $\Lambda.f$, and $O$ is a `JHNeronObjectAtP` for $(p,M,H,\mathfrak{P},\Lambda)$: a scheme $O.G$ over $\operatorname{Spec}$ of the localisation $R_p$ of $\mathbb{Q}$ at $p$, with a relative group law, a bijection $O.\mathrm{pts}$ from $J$ to sections over the generic geometric point, Hecke endomorphisms, and the subgroups $O.\mathrm{toricPts}(m)$ and $O.\mathrm{finPts}(m)$, the latter generated by those $m$-torsion classes whose associated section extends over the place.
--
--   Abel–Jacobi pin. The hypotheses `hD`, `hDQ` state that the designation $(O.G, O.g, \text{unit section})$ represents the relative $\operatorname{Pic}^0$ subfunctor cut out by the fibrewise algebraic-equivalence-to-zero condition `algEquivZeroCut` for the curve `toBase p (ΓM M H) hj` with its section $\mathfrak{X}.\varepsilon_{\inf}$, respectively for its base change to $\mathbb{Q}$ with the base-changed section; `hsep` asserts separatedness of the generic fibre curve. The data `ajQ` (a section of the base-changed designation over the generic curve), `kQ` (a comparison morphism between the two pullbacks of the curve), `ajbar` and `εbar` (a geometric point of $\mathfrak{X}.\mathrm{Meta}.C$ over the base) are tied together by a block of compatibilities, summarised here: `hpoinc` identifies the Poincaré bundle of `hDQ` with the base change of that of `hD`; `hajQε` says the zero section is recovered along $\varepsilon_{\inf}$; `hajQ` expresses, for every field $K$ and every $K$-point $x$ of the generic curve, the pullback of the Poincaré bundle along $x$ followed by `ajQ` as the tensor product of the line bundle of the relative effective Cartier divisor of $x$ with the ideal module of that of the base point; `hkQ₁`, `hkQ₂` fix $k_{\mathbb{Q}}$ over the two projections; `hajbar` defines $\overline{\mathrm{aj}}$ as $\mathfrak{X}.\mathrm{eeta}$ followed by $k_{\mathbb{Q}}$, `ajQ` and the first projection, `hajbar_over` places it over the base, `hεbar` and `hεbar_aj` place $\overline{\varepsilon}$ over the base point and send it to the unit; `hpts_law` says $O.\mathrm{pts}$ is additive for the relative group law obtained from `hD`; and `hAJ` is the Abel–Jacobi identity: for geometric points $x,s$ of $\mathfrak{X}.\mathrm{Meta}.C$ with $s$ lying over $\varepsilon_{\inf}$, there is a degree-zero divisor equal to $(x)-(s)$ under the bijection between points and places whose class satisfies $O.\mathrm{pts}(\text{class}) = x \circ \overline{\mathrm{aj}}$. Finally `hrepΛ` asserts the analogous representability for $\Lambda$ at the level `ΓN p M H hpM`.
--
--   Inertia ring. $R$ is a commutative domain, Henselian local with algebraically closed residue field, a discrete valuation ring (`hRdvr`) in which $p$ is irreducible (`hRirr`), mapping faithfully into $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ with image inside $\mathfrak{P}$ (`hRA`); `hRfix` characterises the inertia subgroup of $\mathfrak{P}$ over $\mathbb{Q}$ as the automorphisms fixing the image of $R$ pointwise, and `hRmax` says every element of $\mathfrak{P}$ fixed by inertia lies in that image.
--
--   Finite-part layer. $\mathcal{G}$ is a $p$-divisible group over $R$ of height $h$ (a system of finite free Hopf algebras $\mathcal{G}.\mathrm{level}\,v$ with surjective transition maps, ranks $p^{vh}$ and kernels the $p^v$-torsion ideals), $\Delta$ an additive map from its $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$-points to $J$, and $e$ a $\mathbb{Z}_p$-linear map on Tate modules. The hypotheses are: `hΔinj` injectivity of $\Delta$; `hΔlev` that $y \in O.\mathrm{finPts}(p^v)$ if and only if $y = \Delta$ of a level-$v$ point; `hΔgal` equivariance of $\Delta$ for automorphisms of $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ over $\mathbb{Q}$ that are $R$-linear; `hΔhecke` that each Hecke generator is realised, through $\Delta$, by a transition-compatible system of $R$-coalgebra endomorphisms of the levels; `he` that $e$ is $\Delta$ applied componentwise, `heinj` its injectivity, `herange` that its range consists of the Tate vectors with $n$-th component in $O.\mathrm{finPts}(p^n)$, `hegal` its Galois equivariance, `hsat` that the range is saturated for multiplication by $p$, `hcoker` that the cokernel of $e$ is $\mathbb{Z}_p$-free of rank $O.\mathrm{toricRank}$, and `htor` that every point of $O.\mathrm{toricPts}(p^v)$ is $\Delta$ of a level-$v$ point.
--
--   Raynaud quotient and scheme-level block. $\mathcal{B}$ is a $p$-divisible group over $R$ of height $h_B$ with coalgebra maps $\psi_v : \mathcal{B}.\mathrm{level}\,v \to \mathcal{G}.\mathrm{level}\,v$, and $h = O.\mathrm{toricRank} + h_B$ (`hhB`), $h_B = 2h'$ (`hhB2`), $\psi$ compatible with transitions (`hψt`). Further: `hψker` says that a level-$v$ point $x$ of $\mathcal{G}$ has trivial image in $\mathcal{B}$ if and only if $\Delta(x) \in O.\mathrm{toricPts}(p^v)$; `hψsurj` surjectivity on points; `hψred` that if the image of $x$ in $\mathcal{B}$ reduces to the identity at $\mathfrak{P}$, in the sense that the $\mathfrak{P}$-valuation of $f(a) - \varepsilon(a)$ is $<1$ for all $a$ in the relevant level, then $x$ itself does; `hperiod` that for $\sigma$ in the inertia subgroup, $z$ a $p^v$-torsion class of $\operatorname{Pic}^0$ and $y$ a level-$v$ point with $\Delta(y) = \sigma z - z$, the image of $y$ in $\mathcal{B}$ reduces to the identity. The scheme-level block consists of a ring homomorphism $\rho_h : R_p \to R$ and morphisms $\iota_v : \operatorname{Spec}(\mathcal{G}.\mathrm{level}\,v) \to O.G$ subject to: `hρh` compatibility of the algebra maps into $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$; `hιbase` that $\iota_v$ lies over $\operatorname{Spec}\rho_h$; `hιcl` that the induced lift to the fibre product is a closed immersion; `hιp` that $\iota_v$ is annihilated by multiplication by $p^v$; `hιpts` that $O.\mathrm{pts}(\Delta(x))$ is computed by composing the point $x$ with $\iota_v$; `hιmul` that $\iota_v$ is multiplicative for the group law; `hιt` compatibility with the transition maps; `hιhecke` that each Hecke generator is realised by a transition-compatible system of coalgebra endomorphisms intertwining $\iota_v$ with the Hecke endomorphism of $O.G$ and acting as `genOpH` through $\Delta$; and `hιfin` that the resulting morphism $j_v$ from $\operatorname{Spec}(\mathcal{G}.\mathrm{level}\,v)$ into the fibre product of the $p^v$-kernel with $\operatorname{Spec}\rho_h$ is both an open and a closed immersion whose image contains every point lying over the closed point of $R$.
--
--   Write $X$ for the image, under the first-component projection [`TateModule.proj p (JH M H) 1`](def/EllipticCurve_TateModule.html#L122) sending $(x_n) \mapsto x_1$, of the additive subgroup underlying the corner submodule $e_{i_0} \cdot T_p J$, where $e_{i_0} = S'.e\,i_0$. Say that $x \in J$ *reduces to the identity* if there is a level-$1$ point $w$ of $\mathcal{G}$ over $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ with $\Delta(w) = x$ such that for every $b \in \mathcal{G}.\mathrm{level}\,1$ the $\mathfrak{P}$-valuation of $w(b) - \varepsilon(b)$, the difference of the value of the corresponding $R$-algebra map at $b$ and the image of the counit of $b$, is $<1$.
--
--   Under these hypotheses the following eight assertions hold. First, every $x$ with $x \in X$ and $x \in O.\mathrm{toricPts}(p)$ satisfies $x \in X$ and reduces to the identity. Second, every $x$ with $x \in X$ which reduces to the identity satisfies $x \in X$ and $x \in O.\mathrm{finPts}(p)$. Third, $0 \in X$ and $0$ reduces to the identity. Fourth, if $x$ and $y$ both lie in $X$ and reduce to the identity, then so does $x + y$, which again lies in $X$. Fifth, if $x$ lies in $X$ and reduces to the identity, then so does $-x$, which again lies in $X$. Sixth, $O.\mathrm{toricPts}(p) \subseteq O.\mathrm{finPts}(p)$. Seventh, every $x \in J$ which is inertia-cyclotomic, that is, such that $\sigma \cdot x = c\,x$ for every $\sigma$ in the inertia subgroup of $\mathfrak{P}$ over $\mathbb{Q}$ and every natural number $c$ with $\sigma\zeta = \zeta^c$ for all $p$-th roots of unity $\zeta$, lies in $O.\mathrm{finPts}(p)$. Eighth, $O.\mathrm{finPts}(p)$ is a finite set.
--
--   This is the bookkeeping step behind the Cartier-duality count on a corner of the Hecke algebra: it records the chain from toric points through the points of the $p$-divisible group reducing to the identity at the place to the finite-part points, the fact that the middle condition defines an additive subgroup, that inertia-cyclotomic $p$-torsion is of finite part, and that the finite part at level $p$ is finite. All clauses are stated without reference to an adjoint corner, so that they apply to either corner, and they are used by the counting statement [`ModularCurve.JHNeronObjectAtP.ncard_corner_finPts_mul_toricPts_eq_ncard_reducesToOne_mul_cyclotomic_of_abelJacobiPin_of_levelData_of_algEquiv`](thm.html#ModularCurve.JHNeronObjectAtP.ncard_corner_finPts_mul_toricPts_eq_ncard_reducesToOne_mul_cyclotomic_of_abelJacobiPin_of_levelData_of_algEquiv).
